<a href="http://gangrey.com/1735">
<h2 class="design">Indifference</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">herd animals</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2008/07/15/AR2008071503150.html?referrer=emailarticle">DeNeen Brown</a> (thanks, Richard): A woman sits alone on a gray chair in a psychiatric ward in a Brooklyn hospital. When we first see her, we do not know how long she has been sitting there. Suddenly, the woman collapses on her face onto the dirty floor. <br />
<br />
We watch through a surveillance camera as she lies there, her blue gown above her knees, her legs convulsing. We watch as a guard comes into the room, puts his hand on his hip, looks at the woman, then looks up at the television hanging from the ceiling. Then the guard walks away. <br />
<br />
We watch as two other patients sit across the room as the woman lies there. We watch them watch her. <br />
<br />
The video, released recently on the Internet, documents the minutes the woman twists on the floor. She stops moving at 6:07 a.m. At 6:35 a.m. a hospital staff member comes in, nudges the patient with her foot. We hope the staffer will do something. But she walks away. <br />
<br />
In the time that passes between action and indifference, between life and death, we wait before someone finally rolls in a blue gurney and oxygen tank, puts the woman on the gurney and rolls her away. Later we learn that Esmin Elizabeth Green, 49, an immigrant from Jamaica who moved to New York to make money to send to her children back home, is dead. <br />
<br />
The camera goes black, leaving its viewers with the question: What might you have done? 
